insert into public.posts (slug, title, excerpt, cover_path, body, category, tags, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, author_id)
values (
  $t$data-centres-africa-power-demand$t$,
  $t$Data centres and Africa's next power demand curve$t$,
  $t$Data centres add large, steady loads in a handful of places. That is a different planning problem from electrification, and it will shape where gas, grid and renewable investment goes next.$t$,
  '/images/cover-power-demand.jpg',
  $json${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A different kind of demand"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A household's demand rises and falls through the day; a data centre's barely moves. A large facility draws close to its peak around the clock and cannot ride through interruptions. The IEA puts the load factor of data centres, the ratio of average to peak load, in the order of 75 to 90%, and notes that operators typically target 99.999% to 99.9999% uptime. Measured against installed capacity the ratio is lower, because computing equipment is installed progressively and operators over-contract grid capacity: the IEA estimates average capacity factors in 2025 of 52% for hyperscale facilities and 47% for colocation. Planners used to building forecasts from electrification and industrial growth now face discrete additions that arrive in blocks, want firm supply and do not respond to time-of-use tariffs."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The demand is also concentrated. Facilities cluster near subsea cable landing points, strong substations and, where cooling needs it, water. Lagos is one such cluster. Estate Intel estimated Nigeria's installed data-centre capacity at 56.1 MW in 2025 and projected more than 218 MW by 2030, and the projects it listed as under construction are all in Lagos. Recent builds show the size of the steps: Rack Centre commissioned its 12 MW LGS2 facility in April 2025; Open Access Data Centres, having filled its first 1.5 MW, began a 24 MW twin facility in Lekki; and in May 2026 Kasi Cloud commissioned a Lekki campus designed to reach about 100 MW of IT capacity. Others are forming across the continent. Concentration strains local networks and gives large operators leverage in tariff negotiations."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Getting connected"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Each large facility forces a decision about the grid. Its load may exceed what the nearest feeder can carry, so the question becomes who pays for the substation or the line: the operator, or every other customer through the tariff. Wheeling terms, the conditions under which power crosses a utility's network to reach a private buyer, move from a technical footnote to the centre of the deal."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Connection capacity is finite. Where a grid is already congested, data centres compete with electrification programmes and industrial users for the same headroom and the same engineering attention. Planners should expect operators to lobby for priority, and to build their own supply when they do not get it."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gas, renewables and firm supply"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Operators can buy from the grid, build captive generation or sign a power purchase agreement. Captive gas-to-power offers control and a predictable cost, at the price of emissions, fuel-supply risk and capital tied up in plant. Renewables with storage can deliver round-the-clock supply, but need long-tenor finance and a grid connection for the balance."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"We have not found a published like-for-like cost comparison for Nigeria, but the components are known. The regulated gas price is low: NMDPRA set the domestic base price of gas for power at $2.18 per MMBtu from 1 April 2026, and $2.68 for commercial users. Reliability is what costs money. The IEA estimates that onsite generation for a 1 GW data centre in an advanced economy must be oversized to between 1.3 and 1.7 GW to match grid reliability, and that even with gas at around $4 per MMBtu the result costs more than industrial grid power in much of the United States; with turbines available 85% of the time rather than 95%, it rises to roughly $180 to $220 per MWh. On the renewable side, Ember modelled 6 GW of solar with 17 GWh of batteries in 12 cities and found the combination could get Abuja 92% of the way to a constant 1 GW supply; in Las Vegas it reached 97% at about $104 per MWh, using global average costs. Finance matters as much as equipment: IRENA's cost analysis for 2024 assumed a cost of capital of 12% in Africa against 3.8% in Europe."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tariff design decides which way operators lean. Where the grid can offer firm supply at a cost-reflective price, a power purchase agreement becomes attractive. Where supply is unreliable or tariffs are administered below cost, captive generation is the rational choice for the operator, even though it duplicates infrastructure and forgoes the grid's economies of scale."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Water, land and cables"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cooling can consume large volumes of water, depending on the technology chosen; in water-stressed regions that becomes a regulatory and reputational issue. Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory estimates that US data centres consumed about 66 billion litres of water on site in 2023, an average of just over 0.36 litres per kWh of IT energy. The spread by technology is wide: systems built on water-cooled chillers without economisers use the most, while air-cooled chillers use no water on site but more energy. Sites therefore follow reliable water, or adopt drier cooling at a higher electrical cost."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Subsea cables set the other anchor. A landing station attracts co-location facilities and the services around them, so each new cable route opens a new candidate region. Coordination between telecoms operators, utilities and landing authorities is rarely designed with that in mind."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Commercial terms and sovereign risk"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Large operators ask for long contracts, fixed or capped tariffs and supply priority. Some of those asks sit uneasily with universal service obligations and with system stability. On the other side, the possibility that a utility cannot pay for the power it buys deters the generators that would serve these loads, which pushes deals towards guarantees from governments or development finance institutions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tariffs that reflect the marginal cost of firm supply let operators weigh their options openly. Tariffs that do not push the same operators towards hidden subsidies or captive plant, and leave utilities carrying the cost."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What to watch"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Connection queues: which projects get capacity, and which are deferred behind other demand."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Captive versus grid: whether new facilities build their own supply, a signal about confidence in the grid."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Water and land constraints in the clusters that are forming."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"New cable landings, each a candidate for the next cluster."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contract terms: tenor, indexation and guarantees, which reveal how sovereign and offtaker risk is being priced."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Storage: whether developers pair renewables with batteries or lock in gas."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sources"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.iea.org/reports/key-questions-on-energy-and-ai"}}],"text":"IEA: Key questions on energy and AI (April 2026)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://nairametrics.com/2025/11/11/nigeria-data-centre-capacity-to-hit-218mw-by-2030-report/"}}],"text":"Nairametrics: Nigeria data centre capacity to hit 218MW by 2030, report (11 November 2025)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://rack-centre.com/rack-centres-lgs2-12mw-data-centre-commissioned/"}}],"text":"Rack Centre: Rack Centre's LGS2 12MW data centre commissioned (April 2025)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.thisdaylive.com/2025/03/24/oadc-to-invest-240m-in-24mw-data-centre-facility-in-lagos/"}}],"text":"ThisDay: OADC to invest $240m in 24MW data centre facility in Lagos (24 March 2025)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://techafricanews.com/2026/05/28/kasi-cloud-datacenters-flags-off-west-africas-first-ai-ready-hyperscale-data-centre-in-lagos/"}}],"text":"TechAfrica News: Kasi Cloud Datacenters flags off West Africa's first AI-ready hyperscale data centre in Lagos (28 May 2026)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.legal500.com/intelligence/nigeria/energy-and-natural-resources/the-nigerian-midstream-and-downstream-petroleum-regulatory-authority-nmdpra-establishes-2026-domestic-base-price-and-wholesale-pricing-framework-for-natural-gas-for-the-strategic-sectors"}}],"text":"Legal500 (Advocaat Law Practice): NMDPRA establishes 2026 domestic base price and wholesale pricing framework for natural gas (7 April 2026)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ember-energy.org/latest-insights/solar-electricity-every-hour-of-every-day-is-here-and-it-changes-everything/"}}],"text":"Ember: Solar electricity every hour of every day is here and it changes everything (21 June 2025)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.irena.org/-/media/Files/IRENA/Agency/Publication/2025/Jul/IRENA_TEC_RPGC_in_2024_Summary_2025.pdf"}}],"text":"IRENA: Renewable power generation costs in 2024 (July 2025)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://eta-publications.lbl.gov/sites/default/files/2024-12/lbnl-2024-united-states-data-center-energy-usage-report_1.pdf"}}],"text":"Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory: 2024 United States data center energy usage report (December 2024)"}]}]}]}]}$json$::jsonb,
  $t$power$t$,
  array[$t$data-centres$t$, $t$power$t$, $t$africa$t$, $t$grid$t$],
  'published',
  '2026-06-23 09:00:00+01'::timestamptz,
  $t$Data centres and Africa's next power demand curve$t$,
  $t$Data centres add large, steady loads in a few places. What that means for grids, gas-to-power, renewables and tariff design across African markets.$t$,
  (select id from public.profiles where role = 'admin' order by created_at limit 1)
)
on conflict (slug) do nothing;

insert into public.posts (slug, title, excerpt, cover_path, body, category, tags, status, published_at, seo_title, seo_description, author_id)
values (
  $t$esg-reporting-african-energy-firms$t$,
  $t$ESG reporting for African energy firms: where to start$t$,
  $t$Lenders and buyers increasingly ask energy companies for measured environmental and social data. A first reporting programme is best built small, on the issues that matter, with the controls to stand behind the numbers.$t$,
  '/images/cover-esg-flare.jpg',
  $json${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why the question is being asked"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Access to capital for energy projects increasingly depends on credible sustainability data. Development banks, commercial lenders and project-finance houses have built ESG assessment into their credit processes, and buyers of energy use the same data to weigh counterparty risk. A firm without measured emissions, documented environmental controls or a record of community engagement is likely to borrow on worse terms, or to be excluded from some pools of capital altogether."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The standards a Nigerian energy borrower is most likely to meet are well established. IFC's eight Performance Standards on Environmental and Social Sustainability, in force since January 2012, govern IFC's own clients and are also applied by other financial institutions. The Equator Principles, in their fourth version since 1 October 2020, set a common baseline for environmental and social risk in project finance; Access Bank and Fidelity Bank are signatories. The African Development Bank's updated Integrated Safeguards System has applied since 31 May 2024. Nigerian banks themselves have been bound since 2012 by the Nigerian Sustainable Banking Principles, launched by the Central Bank of Nigeria with the Bankers' Committee."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That is the commercial case. It does not require a view on the merits of any particular framework."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Start with materiality, not data"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The first step is to decide which issues matter to the business and to the people it depends on: investors, lenders, regulators, communities and customers. For an oil and gas operator that may mean greenhouse gas emissions, flaring, water and community relations. For a renewables developer, land use, grid integration and workforce development may matter more. The list differs by subsector and by site."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A materiality assessment, done once and revisited, decides what gets measured. Firms that skip it tend to build data systems around metrics nobody uses, and to miss the ones a lender asks for first."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Own the data before reporting it"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reporting depends on knowing which site or division produces each number, how it travels to head office, who owns it and how it is checked before it is published. Operational systems hold thousands of daily readings; external reports need a small set of curated, auditable figures. The bridge between the two, whether spreadsheets, a dedicated platform or the finance system, should be documented and controlled."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Weak data architecture shows up as numbers that change between reports, questions from assurance providers that nobody can answer, and operational blind spots."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Emissions in an energy business"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For energy companies the emissions inventory is the hardest part. Scope 1 covers direct emissions: flaring, fugitive methane, fuel burned in generation. Scope 2 covers purchased electricity. Scope 3 covers the value chain, including the use of sold products, which for a producer of fuels is usually the largest category by a wide margin. The IEA estimates that producing, transporting and processing oil and gas caused just under 15% of energy-related greenhouse gas emissions in 2022, and that using them caused another 40%. Seplat Energy's figures show the same shape at company level: its 2025 integrated report gives scope 3 emissions for 2024 of 4.85 million tonnes of CO2-equivalent, almost all from the use of sold products, against 0.60 million tonnes for scope 1 and 2 combined, a ratio of about eight to one, although its scope 3 reporting covers only its onshore operations."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Each scope needs its own method and data. Flaring comes from production records; fugitive methane needs measurement campaigns or estimation models; electricity emissions depend on the grid's carbon intensity; value-chain figures rely on customer data or sector averages. For Nigeria the choice of grid factor matters. Ember's lifecycle estimates put the carbon intensity of Nigerian generation at about 452 grams of CO2-equivalent per kWh in 2025, down from about 496 in 2024 as hydro's share of generation rose. The harmonised default factors used by the international financial institutions (version 3.2, April 2022) give Nigeria a combined-margin factor of about 358 grams of CO2 per kWh for electricity consumption and an operating-margin factor of about 526. A report should state which factor it uses and why. Building this capability is a multi-quarter effort and usually needs outside help at the start."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Credibility and assurance"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Data without assurance is a claim. Third-party assurance, limited at first, signals that methods and controls meet a recognised standard. Many firms have not yet built the audit trails that make assurance possible; doing so early reduces its cost later."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The risk of overstatement runs the other way. Published claims that cannot be substantiated invite challenge, and the cost of a retraction, in financing terms and in reputation, is likely to exceed the cost of the assurance that would have caught it."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Where to start"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Commission a materiality assessment that narrows the field to a handful of issues, with the board and finance leadership in the room."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Map the data behind each issue: source, owner and the route to head office."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Set a baseline year and accept that early numbers will be imperfect but documented."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Choose one framework and document how the material issues map to it. For Nigerian public interest entities the choice is being made for them: under the Financial Reporting Council's roadmap, amended in February 2026, IFRS S1 and S2 are voluntary for periods ending on or before 31 December 2027 and mandatory for periods beginning on or after 1 January 2028, with assurance required from the third year of reporting, and the SEC has told public companies to file implementation plans by 15 October 2026. Do not attempt several at once."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Engage an assurance provider early, for advice on controls before the first audit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Separate the people who produce a number from the people who report it."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sources"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.ifc.org/content/dam/ifc/doc/2023/ifc-performance-standards-2012-en.pdf"}}],"text":"IFC: Performance Standards on Environmental and Social Sustainability (1 January 2012)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://equator-principles.com/about-the-equator-principles/"}}],"text":"Equator Principles Association: About the Equator Principles (accessed September 2026)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://equator-principles.com/report/access-bank-plc-2024/"}}],"text":"Equator Principles Association: Access Bank Plc, 2024 reporting (accessed September 2026)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://equator-principles.com/report/fidelity-bank-plc-2024/"}}],"text":"Equator Principles Association: Fidelity Bank Plc, 2024 reporting (accessed September 2026)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://allafrica.com/stories/202405310083.html"}}],"text":"African Development Bank via allAfrica: Announcement of the effectiveness of the Bank Group's updated Integrated Safeguards System (30 May 2024)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.thisdaylive.com/2023/10/12/sustainable-banking-in-nigeria-a-regulatory-approach/"}}],"text":"ThisDay: Sustainable banking in Nigeria, a regulatory approach (12 October 2023)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.iea.org/reports/emissions-from-oil-and-gas-operations-in-net-zero-transitions"}}],"text":"IEA: Emissions from oil and gas operations in net zero transitions (3 May 2023)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.annualreports.com/HostedData/AnnualReports/PDF/LSE_SEPL_2025.pdf"}}],"text":"Seplat Energy: Integrated Annual Report 2025, sustainability metrics (April 2026)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ember-energy.org/data/yearly-electricity-data/"}}],"text":"Ember: Yearly electricity data (updated September 2026)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/carbon-intensity-electricity"}}],"text":"Our World in Data: Lifecycle carbon intensity of electricity generation, from Ember (updated June 2026)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://unfccc.int/sites/default/files/resource/Harmonized_IFI_Default_Grid_Factors_2021_v3.2_0.xlsx"}}],"text":"IFI Technical Working Group (UNFCCC): Harmonized IFI default grid factors, version 3.2 (April 2022)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://frcnigeria.gov.ng/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Roadmap-Report-for-the-Adoption-of-IFRS-Sustainability-Disclosure-Standards-in-Nigeria-Amended-2026-.pdf"}}],"text":"Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria: Roadmap report for the adoption of IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standards in Nigeria, amended 2026 (February 2026)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://frcnigeria.gov.ng/2026/02/26/frc-unveils-amended-roadmap-and-sustainability-reporting-guideline-to-strengthen-adoption-of-ifrs-sustainability-disclosure-standards-in-nigeria/"}}],"text":"Financial Reporting Council of Nigeria: FRC unveils amended roadmap and sustainability reporting guideline (26 February 2026)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://sec.gov.ng/for-investors/keep-track-of-circulars/implementation-of-the-ifrs-sustainability-disclosure-standards-in-nigeria/"}}],"text":"Securities and Exchange Commission, Nigeria: Implementation of the IFRS Sustainability Disclosure Standards in Nigeria (23 September 2026)"}]}]}]}]}$json$::jsonb,
